.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt NEWFS_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm newfs_minixfs
.Nd make a MINIX file system, empty or from a directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl V Ar version
.Op Fl N
.Op Fl B Cm le | be
.Op Fl b Ar block-size
.Oo
.Fl d Ar directory
.Op Fl F Ar specfile Op Fl P Ar dbdir Op Fl x
.Op Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
.Oc
.Op Fl e Cm 0 | 1
.Op Fl i Ar inodes
.Op Fl l Ar name-length | Cm flex
.Op Fl m Cm minix | linux | Ar bytes
.Op Fl s Ar blocks
.Op Fl t Ar time
.Op Fl W Ar bits
.Op Fl z Ar log-zone-size
.Ar image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes a MINIX file system of version 1, 2 or 3, or one of Minix-vmd, in
the file or device
.Ar image ;
see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
It holds an empty root directory or, with
.Fl d ,
a copy of a directory.
The first 1024 bytes of
.Ar image ,
where a boot block may be, are left alone.
The file system is marked clean.
The image is locked with
.Xr fcntl 2
while it is written, and one that another program holds is refused.
.Pp
The size comes from
.Fl s ,
which also makes the image file or cuts it to that size, or else from
the size of an existing file.
With
.Fl d
and neither, the image is made just large enough for the tree.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl V Ar version
The version of the file system: 1, 2 or 3.
It has to be given.
.It Fl N
Print the layout, and with
.Fl d
what the tree needs, and write nothing.
.It Fl B Cm le | be
Byte order: little-endian (default) or big-endian.
.It Fl b Ar block-size
The block size of V3, a multiple of 1024 up to 32768; 4096 by default,
the smallest that MINIX 3 mounts.
V1 and V2 always have blocks of 1024 bytes.
.It Fl d Ar directory
Copy the directory tree in name order, preserving modes, owners, times
and hard links; blocks of zeros become holes.
The image root inherits the source directory's attributes.
Symbolic links use mode 0777; sockets are skipped with a warning.
Device files can be copied without root privileges.
.Pp
Before writing, check name lengths, IDs, device numbers, link counts and
capacity, counting holes as data.
Report all problems and write nothing if any are found.
Unless
.Fl i
is given, allocate enough inodes for the tree.
.It Fl F Ar specfile
Apply an
.Xr mtree 8
specification in hierarchical or full-path (METALOG) form.
Entries override the source files' attributes; a type mismatch is an error.
Missing files are created unless marked
.Cm optional .
Creation requires type, mode, owner and group, plus a symbolic link target
or device number where applicable; regular files are created empty.
.Pp
Supported keywords are
.Cm type , mode
(octal),
.Cm uid , uname , gid , gname , time , link , device
and
.Cm optional .
Checksums,
.Cm size , flags , tags
and other keywords are ignored.
.Cm /set , /unset ,
continued lines and
.Xr vis 3
escapes are supported; name patterns are not.
.It Fl P Ar dbdir
Look up the user names of
.Fl F
in
.Pa master.passwd
or
.Pa passwd ,
and the group names in
.Pa group ,
of the directory
.Ar dbdir ,
rather than in those of the system.
.It Fl x
Copy only what the specification of
.Fl F
names.
.It Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
Give every file of the tree the owner
.Ar uid
and the group
.Ar gid ,
numbers both.
A V1 inode keeps its group in one byte, so a tree with groups above 255
needs this in V1.
.It Fl e Cm 0 | 1
The bits of the maps past the last inode and zone: 0, as MINIX 2 and
later leave them, or 1, as MINIX 1 and Linux set them; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
The default is 1 for names of 30 characters and 0 otherwise.
.It Fl i Ar inodes
The number of inodes, rounded up to fill the blocks of the inode table;
by default one for about every three blocks, or more if the tree of
.Fl d
needs them.
.It Fl l Ar name-length | Cm flex
Select the V1 or V2 directory format: 14-character MINIX names (default),
30-character Linux names, or
.Cm flex
for Minix-vmd names of up to 60 characters.
V3 always uses 60-character names.
Format details are in
.Xr minixfs 5 ;
map padding defaults are described under
.Fl e .
.It Fl m Cm minix | linux | Ar bytes
Set the maximum file size:
.Cm minix
uses the range reachable through double indirect zones;
.Cm linux
uses Linux's limit (2147483647 bytes in V2 and V3);
.Ar bytes
specifies a value from 1 to 2147483647.
The default is
.Cm linux
for 30-character names and
.Cm minix
otherwise.
Linux and these tools enforce this limit.
See
.Xr minixfs 5
for MINIX compatibility restrictions.
.It Fl s Ar blocks
The size in blocks.
.It Fl t Ar time
The time of the root directory, in seconds since the Epoch, for images
that must come out the same each time.
.It Fl W Ar bits
Write the bit maps of a big-endian file system as words of
.Ar bits
bits, 8, 16, 32 or 64, instead of 16 for V1 and V2 and 8 for V3; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.It Fl z Ar log-zone-size
A zone of 2 to the power of
.Ar log-zone-size
blocks; 0, one block, by default.
MINIX 3 after 3.3.0 does not mount a file system with zones of more
than one block; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if the file system cannot be made, and 2 for
a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A V1 floppy of 360K and a V3 image of 64 megabytes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 1 -s 360 floppy.img
$ newfs_minixfs -V 3 -s 16384 disk.img
.Ed
.Pp
An image of a directory, just large enough, owned by root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 2 -d tree -o 0:0 tree.img
.Ed
.Pp
An image of a MINIX build, with the owners, modes and devices of its
METALOG:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ newfs_minixfs -V 3 -d destdir -F destdir/METALOG \e
    -P destdir/etc -x usr.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr makefs 8 ,
.Xr mtree 8 ,
.Xr tunefs_minixfs 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
